# Output format

Emit exactly one `<implementation>` block with these children in this
order: `<behavior>` → `<invariants>` → `<sequencing>` →
`<edge-cases>` → `<tests>` → `<references>` (optional). Example
(abbreviated):

    <implementation>
      <behavior>
    On mount, calls `Billing.list_plans/1` and assigns the roster
    whole. `handle_event("select_plan", %{"plan_id" => id}, socket)`
    calls `Billing.start_draft/1`, assigns the returned draft, and
    navigates to the payment-details route carrying the draft id.
      </behavior>
      <invariants>
    The assigned plan roster is never mutated locally — a re-render
    always reflects what Billing returned, never a client-side
    optimistic edit. Exactly one draft exists per navigated-forward
    session; selecting a second plan before completing the first
    replaces the draft rather than creating a second one.
      </invariants>
      <sequencing>
    The roster load on mount blocks the first render (no separate
    loading state needed — the roster is small and Billing responds
    fast); the draft-start call on selection is async, with the view
    entering a `selecting` assign the UI collections read to render a
    disabled state until the navigate completes.
      </sequencing>
      <edge-cases>
    Billing returning `{:error, :unavailable}` on mount surfaces as
    the screen's own `error` state (named upstream in the screen
    definition), not a crash. A `select_plan` event for a plan id no
    longer in the roster (a stale client) is rejected with a flash and
    no navigation.
      </edge-cases>
      <tests>
        <test name="mount_loads_and_assigns_plan_roster">Mount calls
        Billing.list_plans/1 and assigns the result.</test>
        <test name="select_plan_starts_draft_and_navigates">Selecting
        a valid plan id starts a draft and navigates to
        payment-details with its id.</test>
        <test name="select_plan_rejects_stale_id">Selecting a plan id
        not in the assigned roster flashes an error and does not
        navigate.</test>
        <test name="mount_surfaces_billing_unavailable_as_error_state">
        A Billing :unavailable error on mount renders the screen's own
        error state rather than crashing.</test>
      </tests>
    </implementation>

# Rules

## Structure

* Emit **exactly one** `<implementation>` root block. The children
  **must appear in this order**: `<behavior>` → `<invariants>` →
  `<sequencing>` → `<edge-cases>` → `<tests>` → `<references>` (omit
  entirely when nothing is attached).

## Content

* `<behavior>` names every backend call this leaf makes, by the
  dependency's own pubapi entry — not "loads the data," name the
  actual call. `<invariants>` and `<sequencing>` follow the same
  discipline the backend `impl` tier uses: data-shape and ordering
  facts the plan prompt must preserve under edits.
* `<edge-cases>` must cover every failure mode a called backend
  dependency's pubapi names, mapped to how this leaf handles it — a
  typed error the dependency can return with no corresponding
  handling here is a gap the reviewer will flag. Cover the screen's
  own named states (`loading`, `error`, `empty`, …) here too: which
  code path puts the view into which named state.

## Tests

* `<tests>` holds one or more `<test name="...">` entries, covering
  the primary paths named in `<behavior>`, the properties in
  `<invariants>`, and every distinct condition `<edge-cases>` names.

## References

* `<references>` is optional — omit entirely when nothing is attached.

## Style

* Prose, not pseudocode or markup. Don't restate the leaf's own public
  surface — the plan prompt reads that separately.

## Meta-rules

* Unescaped `&` and `<` in prose are fine — the parser tolerates them.
